WEBVTT

NOTE
Editorial caption timing for the 12:30 production script.
Re-align cue boundaries to the final recorded waveform without changing words.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Ontologies are knowledge spaces.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.500
A database can hold facts.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.500
Search can find a passage.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:17.500
Memory can bring an old conversation back.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:25.500
But none of those things, by themselves,
tell an agent what kind of thing it found,

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:34.500
what it connects to, whether it is still current,
or what it is allowed to do with it.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:40.000
A knowledge space models a small,
deliberate part of a world:

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:48.000
the concepts that matter, the relationships between them,
how those relationships change over time,
and how much confidence we place in them.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Now imagine a team preparing to announce
a new community program.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.000
The partners are ready. The message is scheduled.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
People have already begun arranging
their work around it.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Then someone pauses and asks,
“Are we actually allowed to go ahead?”

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:17.000
The agent searches meeting notes, decision logs,
old conversations, and project records.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Somewhere in that history,
there should be an answer.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
And then it finds one: an official record,
from a genuine source,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:42.000
carrying one very reassuring word—approved.
At first, this feels like success.
The agent found the answer.
But did it find permission today—or evidence from the past?

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The approval was genuine. It was not fabricated.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.000
But its validity period had ended.
A later review might supersede it.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:04.000
And the evidence we selected did not establish
a current decision from the person who now owns the program.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Nothing here makes the old record false.
It changes what we are justified in doing with it.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
This is where time, confidence, and authority
have to remain separate.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Time tells us when a statement is meant to hold.
Confidence tells us how strongly the evidence supports it.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Authority tells us who may establish
or change the governed state.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:39.000
The ontology did not vote yes or no.
It made the missing premises visible.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.000
The safer answer is not simply “approved.” It is:

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:50.000
“I found a genuine earlier approval.
I cannot establish that it is current.
I should ask the responsible owner—or stop.”

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:57.000
A small example from another ontology
makes this separation easier to see.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:06.000
In the Body War model, a score preview can become available
after a calculation succeeds.
But available does not mean promoted.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:15.000
The evidence behind that preview may carry medium confidence
while an owner’s commitment remains low.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:25.000
Those statements do not contradict each other.
They answer different questions.
Confidence describes support.
Promotion describes governed status.
Keeping them separate prevents evidence
from quietly turning into commitment.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Now return to the earlier approval
and put the world back around it.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:41.000
An ontology gives the missing questions a place.
This is a decision. It applies to this program,

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:50.000
came from this source, held during this period,
may be superseded by a later review,
and remains owned here.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:59.000
CyberAlchemy v2 sharpens that contract.
Meaning arrives with evidence, provenance, time, confidence,

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:07.000
lifecycle, ownership, and rules for use.
This is not a perfect digital copy of reality.
It is a bounded model for a purpose.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:20.000
A glossary can name the concepts.
A taxonomy can group them.
A knowledge space also shows how they interact,
how they change, and what follows.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Ontology diagrams often look like boxes and arrows.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.000
But the meaning is carried by the verb on the arrow.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:40.000
In one DomainSpec model, a rule enforces an operation.
“Enforces” fixes the source kind, target kind,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.000
direction, and obligation.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.000
In our story, a decision applies to a program.
It is owned by a decision-maker.
A later review may supersede it.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Change the verb, reverse the direction,
or connect the wrong kinds of things,
and the agent is no longer interpreting the same world.
Malformed connections become testable.
But a valid semantic relation does not prove
that a runtime obeys the model;
runtime conformance needs separate evidence.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:17.000
Now follow meaning through a series of handoffs.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:26.000
We begin with an owned source.
We select evidence from it.
That evidence may support a candidate interpretation.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:35.000
The candidate can be brought to the responsible owner.
If the owner admits it, it becomes governed meaning.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:43.000
Other systems may then receive a view
prepared for a particular use.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:52.000
It is tempting to see this as a conveyor belt,
as though everything naturally moves to the right.
It does not.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:59.000
Each handoff answers a different question,
and one transition is a real authority gate.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:07.000
The ontology can show where the gate is.
It can name the owner.
It can show what evidence and conditions
the decision depends on.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:16.000
It can reveal that the request is incomplete.
But the map does not acquire the owner’s authority
simply because it describes the route accurately.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:25.000
A useful projection is not automatically authoritative.
A well-formed candidate is not automatically admitted.
And a request that reaches the right person
is still not approval.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:34.000
An agent cannot carry the whole organization
into every task.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:43.000
More context would not automatically create better judgment.
It needs the right slice of the world for this question.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:50.000
That lets us separate five roles
that are often blurred together.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Memory retains candidate material:
the old decision, the later review,
and a conversation that may matter.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:06.000
A context builder selects a bounded packet
and preserves where each piece came from.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:16.000
The ontology supplies the meaning contract:
this is a decision; it applies to this scope;
it had this validity period;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.000
this review may supersede it;
this owner controls reliance.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:28.000
The owner makes the authority decision.
Only then does a runtime act
and produce evidence about what happened.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:40.000
Keeping those roles separate lets the system explain
what it found, why it was selected, what it means,
what remains uncertain, and who decides next.
The packet is a task view—not a new source of truth.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:47.000
Return to the team’s question:
“Are we allowed to go ahead?”

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.000
The safer answer is precise.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:08:00.000
“I found a genuine earlier approval.
Its validity period has ended.
A later review may supersede it,

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:08.000
and I cannot establish a current decision
from the responsible owner.
I should not treat the old record
as permission to proceed.”

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:20.000
The agent can now ask for a current decision—or stop
before the announcement.
That is not less intelligence.
It is intelligence that can explain
what its answer depends on.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:28.000
This is the idea I am making concrete
with the ontology for Augmentor.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:40.000
Think of Augmentor as a bridge between material
an organization already owns and the bounded context
an agent needs for one piece of work.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:09:00.000
In the upper lane, an owned source can be indexed temporarily.
The index helps find relevant material
and assemble a context snapshot.
But the index is disposable.
It does not become the new owner of the source,
and the snapshot does not become the whole truth.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:22.000
In the lower lane, the system may notice
a pattern worth remembering.
It can form a proposed memory,
send it through provider review,
reconnect it to its source,
and express an ontology candidate.
Then it can prepare a request
for the responsible owner gate.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:27.000
Look closely at where that arrow stops.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:45.000
Augmentor may retrieve material.
It may preserve provenance.
It may build a temporary index,
compose bounded context,
and form a well-typed candidate.
It may identify which owner needs to decide next.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:52.000
What it cannot do is turn
its own candidate into permission.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:10:12.000
In the ontology package, that boundary is explicit:
the authority effect is none.
This is not a weakness hidden in the fine print.
It is what allows Augmentor to be useful
without quietly becoming a second authority system.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:24.000
And the evidence boundary matters.
This ontology makes the intended design inspectable.
It does not, by itself, prove that durable memory,
live routing, reasoning, automated promotion,
deployment, or production release already exists.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:35.000
The promise is narrower and more important:
carry enough meaning into the task,
preserve the path back to its owners,
and stop at the gate that only an owner may cross.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:43.000
Step back, and the whole movement
becomes simple enough to remember.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:52.000
An owned source keeps the record.
Retrieval finds candidate evidence.
Selection chooses what matters for this task.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:11:01.000
The ontology types its meaning and relationships.
A context builder packages the bounded view
with provenance and warnings.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:10.000
A named owner decides whether reliance is permitted.
Only then does a separate runtime act
and bring back evidence from reality.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:17.000
Each step answers a different question.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:23.000
What exists? What is relevant? What does it mean?
May we rely on it? And what actually happened?

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:34.000
A good agent should be able to communicate that chain:
what it found, where it came from,
how it interpreted it, what remains uncertain,
and which decision belongs to someone else.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:40.000
Find candidate evidence. Type its meaning.
Bound the context. Gate its use.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:49.000
The goal is not an agent that always answers.
It is an agent that knows what its answer depends on.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:58.000
An ontology gives remembered information
a world around it.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:07.000
It makes concepts, relationships, time, confidence,
provenance, lifecycle, and ownership
available to interpretation.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:16.000
Augmentor is a way to carry a bounded piece
of that world into an agent’s work
while preserving the path back to its owners.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:20.000
Authority remains with the people and systems
that actually own the decision.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:30.000
The map can make the route visible.
It can help us ask a much better question.
But the map does not grant permission.
